<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">African Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The African Union is the continental organisation of the 55 states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, founded in 2002 as the successor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> of 1963. Based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, it promotes political and economic integration, has a Pan-African Parliament and a Peace and Security Council, and has sent peacekeeping missions, notably to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> rejoined it in 2017.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/african-union.jpg" alt="African Union" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: African Union - supranational union in Africa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_African_Union.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The historical foundations of the African Union lie in the First Congress of Independent African States, held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, from 15 to 22 April 1958, which aimed to establish Africa Day to mark the African peoples' wish to free themselves from colonial rule. Later attempts at unity included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a>, established on 25 May 1963 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> by 32 signatory governments, and the African Economic Community in 1991. Critics argued that the Organisation did little to protect citizens' rights from their own political leaders and dubbed it the Dictators' Club.</p><p>The Sirte Declaration of 9 September 1999 called for an African Union. The bloc was launched on 9 July 2002 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> was disbanded that day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The most important decisions are made by the Assembly, a meeting of the heads of state and government of its member states. The Pan-African Parliament sits at Midrand, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, and its secretariat, the African Union Commission, is based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, in a headquarters complex inaugurated on 28 January 2012 and built as a gift from the Chinese government. The Peace and Security Council can authorise peace support missions and impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> after an unconstitutional change of government, and the Constitutive Act allows intervention in grave circumstances: war crimes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> and crimes against humanity.</p><p>All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> member states on the African continent belong to the Union, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahrawi-arab-democratic-republic">Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic</a>. Morocco withdrew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> in 1984 over the Sahrawi Republic's admission and was readmitted on 30 January 2017.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Union's objectives include greater unity and solidarity, the defence of its members' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and territorial integrity, and the integration of the continent. At its July 2007 summit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> the principal topic was a Union Government, aiming at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and member states divided between those who wanted a common government with an army and those who preferred strengthening existing structures. In 2018 the Union adopted a Free Movement Protocol. Its emblem shows the silhouette of Africa drawn without internal borders to symbolise unity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “African Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It belongs to the Arab League, the Arab Maghreb Union and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation; it left the Organisation of African Unity in 1984 when the Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic was admitted and rejoined the <b>African Union</b> in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was a founding member of the United Nations, the Non-Aligned Movement, the Arab League, the <b>African Union</b> and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, and is a member of BRICS.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its claim to independence rests on the borders of the former British protectorate and on its brief existence as a sovereign state in 1960; the <b>African Union</b>, however, has been wary of any change to colonial-era borders, and most governments support the territorial integrity of Somalia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…army, later renamed the Uganda People’s Defence Force, which fought the Lord’s Resistance Army of Joseph Kony in the north for some two decades, intervened in the Congo wars from 1996 to 2003, and since 2007 has provided the largest contingent of the <b>African Union</b> mission in Somalia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It is a working language of the East African Community and the <b>African Union</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/swahili">Swahili · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sixth Pan-African Congress, the first held in Africa, met in Dar es Salaam in 1974, and in 2002 the <b>African Union</b> replaced the OAU.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
